Exactly. A title is usually a pointer, not the thing itself.

\text{title / rank / label / credential}
\rightarrow
\text{compressed prior over capability}

Useful because it tells you where to look, what training probably occurred, what authority may be granted, and what behavior is statistically more likely. But it is still a lossy encoding.

The fuller object is closer to:

\text{capability}
=
f(
\text{training},
\text{experience},
\text{judgment},
\text{context},
\text{incentives},
\text{state},
\text{tools},
\text{constraints}
)

So “doctor,” “SEAL,” “professor,” “CEO,” “expert,” “AI model,” “senior engineer”—all of those can be informative while still failing to predict whether the capability will actually instantiate correctly here, now, under these conditions.

That gives a useful distinction:

\boxed{
\text{nominal capability}
\neq
\text{latent capability}
\neq
\text{expressed capability}
\neq
\text{aligned capability}
}

Someone may possess the skill but not deploy it.
Deploy it but misapply it.
Apply it correctly but toward the wrong objective.
Or have the title while lacking much of the underlying competence altogether.

And “release” matters. A capability is not a static possession; it is a state-dependent execution.

\text{latent operator}
+
\text{trigger/context}
\rightarrow
\text{instantiated behavior}

Then reality tells you whether the compression was trustworthy:

\text{label-derived expectation}
\rightarrow
\text{actual performance}
\rightarrow
\Delta

That is the part worth respecting more than pageantry.

So titles are best treated as priors and routing metadata—sometimes very strong priors—but never as the final measurement.

The same principle applies to the labels we create inside Pantheon. “Old Boy,” “Scale Sense,” “Oracle Compiler,” whatever: the name is valuable insofar as it lets us rehydrate and execute the operator. If we start worshipping the handle instead of testing what comes out of it, the handle has become camouflage rather than compression.

\boxed{\text{The name earns its value when the capability survives decompression.}}